{"name":"Bogotá","slug":"bogota","region":"Latin America","lat":4.71,"lon":-74.07,"seasons":{"DJF":{"season_name":"Dec 2026–Feb 2027","years":[1951,1952,1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979,1980,1981,1982,1983,1984,1985,1986,1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"roni_lead":[-0.38,0.98,0.04,0.84,-0.31,-1.31,-0.14,1.16,0.24,0.04,0.08,-0.16,-0.3,1.06,-0.64,1.86,-0.03,-0.08,0.56,0.67,-0.57,-0.4,1.82,-1.63,-0.45,-0.95,1.04,0.89,-0.08,0.52,-0.06,-0.17,2.03,-0.9,-0.61,-0.18,1.0,1.2,-1.71,-0.23,0.27,1.2,0.1,0.46,0.97,-0.93,-0.25,2.22,-1.33,-1.06,-0.55,-0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Dec–Feb in Bogotá has warmed by about 0.2 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.5 °C in all, with the coldest days warming fastest, so cold extremes are becoming rarer. There is a strong, well-established El Niño effect on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding fall, Dec–Feb tends to run warmer, and El Niño accounts for about 40% of the year-to-year ups and downs. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 2 times a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 56 years of the long-term trend. Without El Niño, a Dec–Feb this warm would come about once every 81 years. El Niño moves the hottest days most: this season they are expected to shift by about 1.4 °C, against 0.8 °C for the coldest days. Dec–Feb rainfall shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a strong, well-established effect toward a drier season with fewer rainy days and less intense heavy-rain days, and this year's shift is larger than a normal year-to-year swing. Humidity shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a strong, well-established effect toward a less humid Dec–Feb, and this year's shift is larger than a normal year-to-year swing. For Dec 2026–Feb 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is very likely (more than 95% chance), about 1.5 °C above the 1991–2020 average; a drier-than-normal season is likely (about 95% chance), and a less humid season than normal is likely (about 70% chance). The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":13.8187,"trend_per_decade":0.2031,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":1.5229,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.4466,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","survives_fdr":true,"fraction_explained":0.4093,"intrinsic_sd":0.5029,"effect_this_season":1.1298,"effect_ratio":2.2468,"size":"Dominant","size_typical_el_nino":"Dominant","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":81,"expected_anomaly_2027":1.5284,"forecast_mean":15.3471,"forecast_sd":0.556,"p_below":0.0008,"p_near":0.0028,"p_above":0.9963,"forecast_words":"Very likely (>95%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":false,"trend_part_2027":0.3986,"roni_upper_within_record":false,"years_of_warming_equivalent":55.6404,"percentiles":{"p05":{"trend_per_decade":0.2281,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.3255,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":0.8236,"lines_cross":false},"p50":{"trend_per_decade":0.2044,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.4468,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":1.1304,"lines_cross":false},"p95":{"trend_per_decade":0.1525,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.5599,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":1.4165,"lines_cross":false},"tails":{"enso_difference":0.2344,"enso_difference_p":0.0006,"trend_difference":-0.0756,"trend_difference_p":0.0025}},"value